Below is a practical “tool‑kit” you can copy‑paste into your MapLibre GL JS app to make any point‑icon pop no matter what the satellite/aerial backdrop looks like.

---

## 1. The two most reliable tricks

| Trick | What it does | When to use it |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **Icon halo (stroke)** | Draws a thin outline around the whole sprite (the same way a text halo works). | You already have a clean, single‑color PNG/SVG sprite. |
| **Background “halo” circle** | Renders a solid‑color circle (or any shape) *under* the icon, acting like a “pin‑head”. | Your sprite has transparent padding or you want a bigger visual anchor (e.g., a white disc behind a green leaf). |

You can use **either** one, or **both** (circle + halo) for maximum contrast.

---

## 2. Icon halo – the built‑in style spec

MapLibre’s `symbol` layer supports the same halo properties that text does:

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'my-points',
  type: 'symbol',
  source: 'my-points-source',
  layout: {
    // keep the icon upright and on top of other symbols
    'icon-allow-overlap': true,
    'icon-ignore-placement': true,
    // optional: show a label next to the icon
    // 'text-field': ['get', 'name'],
    // 'text-offset': [0, 1.2],
    // 'text-anchor': 'top',
  },
  paint: {
    // ---- ICON ----
    // the actual colour of the sprite (only works if the sprite is a monochrome PNG/SVG)
    'icon-color': '#00ff00',               // your green or brown base colour
    // a thin, high‑contrast outline
    'icon-halo-color': '#ffffff',          // white works on almost everything
    'icon-halo-width': 2,                  // 1‑3 px is usually enough
    // optional: make the halo a little softer
    'icon-halo-blur': 0.5,

    // ---- OPTIONAL LABEL ----
    // 'text-color': '#000000',
    // 'text-halo-color': '#ffffff',
    // 'text-halo-width': 2,
  }
});
```

### Why it works

* The halo is drawn **outside** the sprite’s opaque pixels, so even a fully‑transparent PNG will get a crisp white ring.
* The halo colour can be any high‑contrast hue (white, black, yellow). If you need a dark outline for bright imagery, just flip it:

```js
'icon-halo-color': '#000000',
'icon-halo-width': 2,
```

* The halo width is in **device‑independent pixels**, not map units, so it stays the same size at any zoom.

---

## 3. Background circle (or any shape) under the icon

If you want a bigger “anchor” that guarantees visibility (think of the classic Google‑Maps red pin), add a **second symbol layer** that draws a solid circle *behind* the icon.

```js
// 1️⃣ Circle layer – drawn first (under everything else)
map.addLayer({
  id: 'my-points-bg',
  type: 'circle',
  source: 'my-points-source',
  paint: {
    // radius in map units (pixels at the current zoom)
    'circle-radius': 12,               // make it a little larger than the icon
    'circle-color': '#ffffff',         // white works on most backgrounds
    'circle-stroke-color': '#000000',  // optional thin black border
    'circle-stroke-width': 1,
    // optional: a subtle drop‑shadow for extra depth
    'circle-blur': 0.5,
    'circle-opacity': 0.9
  }
});

// 2️⃣ Icon layer – drawn on top of the circle
map.addLayer({
  id: 'my-points-icon',
  type: 'symbol',
  source: 'my-points-source',
  layout: {
    'icon-image': ['get', 'icon'],   // name of the sprite in your sprite sheet
    'icon-size': 1,
    'icon-allow-overlap': true,
    'icon-ignore-placement': true
  },
  paint: {
    // you can still keep the halo if you like
    'icon-color': '#00ff00',
    'icon-halo-color': '#ffffff',
    'icon-halo-width': 1
  }
});
```

### How to pick the right radius & colour

| Situation | Recommended circle colour | Radius (px) |
|-----------|--------------------------|-------------|
| Dark forest / water | **White** (`#ffffff`) | 10‑14 |
| Bright desert / snow | **Black** (`#000000`) | 10‑14 |
| Mixed urban‑rural | **Yellow** (`#ffeb3b`) or **orange** (`#ff9800`) | 12‑16 |
| Want a “pin” look | Use a **transparent PNG** with a coloured border instead of a circle layer. |

You can also **data‑driven** the colour per feature:

```js
'circle-color': [
  'match',
  ['get', 'type'],
  'tree',   '#ffffff',
  'building', '#ffeb3b',
  /* default */ '#cccccc'
]
```

---

## 4. Combining both tricks (the “ultimate safety net”)

```js
// Circle background (white)
map.addLayer({ /* as above */ });

// Icon with halo (white outline)
map.addLayer({
  id: 'my-points-icon',
  type: 'symbol',
  source: 'my-points-source',
  layout: { /* same as before */ },
  paint: {
    'icon-color': '#00ff00',
    'icon-halo-color': '#ffffff',
    'icon-halo-width': 2,
    'icon-halo-blur': 0.5
  }
});
```

Result: a white disc + a white outline → the icon is **always** visible, even if the disc itself blends (e.g., over a snowfield). The double‑outline trick is cheap (just two layers) and works on any device.

---

## 5. If you prefer to bake the outline into the sprite

Sometimes you don’t want to rely on the style‑spec halo (e.g., you need a thick, coloured border that changes per‑icon). In that case:

1. **Create a 2‑layer PNG/SVG** – a solid coloured shape (green/brown) **inside** a transparent ring of the colour you want for the outline (white, black, etc.).
2. Export it at **2×** the size you’ll display it (to keep it crisp on Hi‑DPI screens).
3. Add it to your sprite sheet and reference it with `'icon-image': 'my‑icon‑with‑stroke'`.

*Pros*: works even on older Mapbox‑GL‑compatible renderers that don’t support `icon-halo-*`.  
*Cons*: you need a separate sprite for each colour/outline combination.

---

## 6. Performance & ordering tips

| Tip | Why it matters |
|-----|----------------|
| **Place the background circle layer **below** the icon layer** (`addLayer` order). | Guarantees the icon is on top and not hidden by the circle. |
| **Set `icon-allow-overlap: true` and `icon-ignore-placement: true`** if you have many points close together. | Prevents the engine from trying to hide overlapping symbols, which can cause the “vanish” effect you described. |
| **Keep the circle radius modest (≤ 16 px)**. | Larger circles increase the number of pixels drawn per frame, which can affect frame‑rate on mobile. |
| **Use `circle-pitch-alignment: 'map'`** if you want the circles to stay flat on the globe (default). | Prevents distortion when the map is pitched. |
| **If you have a huge dataset, consider clustering** and only show the detailed icons at higher zoom levels. | Reduces the number of symbols rendered at once. |

---

## 7. Full minimal example (copy‑paste)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – icons that never disappear</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // Satellite imagery (replace with your own source)
        'satellite': {
          type: 'raster',
          tiles: [
            'https://services.arcgisonline.com/ArcGIS/rest/services/World_Imagery/MapServer/tile/{z}/{y}/{x}'
          ],
          tileSize: 256,
          attribution: '© Esri'
        },
        // Point data – GeoJSON with a property `icon` that matches a sprite name
        'points': {
          type: 'geojson',
          data: {
            type: 'FeatureCollection',
            features: [
              { type: 'Feature', geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [-122.45, 37.78] }, properties: { icon: 'green-tree' } },
              { type: 'Feature', geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [-122.46, 37.77] }, properties: { icon: 'brown-rock' } }
            ]
          }
        }
      },
      layers: [
        { id: 'satellite', type: 'raster', source: 'satellite' },

        // 1️⃣ Background circle (white)
        {
          id: 'point-bg',
          type: 'circle',
          source: 'points',
          paint: {
            'circle-radius': 12,
            'circle-color': '#ffffff',
            'circle-stroke-color': '#000000',
            'circle-stroke-width': 1,
            'circle-opacity': 0.9
          }
        },

        // 2️⃣ Icon with halo
        {
          id: 'point-icons',
          type: 'symbol',
          source: 'points',
          layout: {
            'icon-image': ['get', 'icon'],
            'icon-size': 1,
            'icon-allow-overlap': true,
            'icon-ignore-placement': true
          },
          paint: {
            'icon-color': '#00ff00',          // green – change per‑icon if you like
            'icon-halo-color': '#ffffff',
            'icon-halo-width': 2,
            'icon-halo-blur': 0.5
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [-122.45, 37.78],
    zoom: 13,
    pitch: 45,
    bearing: -17
  });

  // Load a custom sprite (make sure it contains `green-tree` and `brown-rock`)
  map.on('style.load', () => {
    map.addImage('green-tree', new Image(), { sdf: true }); // placeholder – replace with real image
    map.addImage('brown-rock', new Image(), { sdf: true });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

> **Note** – The `sdf: true` flag tells MapLibre that the sprite is a **signed‑distance‑field** image, which lets `icon-color` and `icon-halo-*` work on any colour you set at runtime. If you use ordinary PNGs, you can still use the halo, but you won’t be able to recolour the sprite with `icon-color`. For maximum flexibility, export your icons as **SDF** (e.g., using Mapbox’s `spritezero` tool or the `@mapbox/spritezero` npm package).

