We should answer clearly.

Below is a complete, ready‑to‑copy example that shows how to **fly** the MapLibre GL JS camera to a new location when the user clicks a button.  
It uses the `map.flyTo()` method, which animates the transition (smooth “fly‑to” effect) and lets you control speed, easing, zoom, bearing, pitch, etc.

---

## 1. Basic HTML

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre GL – Fly To on Button Click</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">

  <!-- MapLibre GL CSS -->
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.2/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />

  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; left:0; right:0; }
    #controls {
      position: absolute;
      top: 10px;
      left: 10px;
      background: rgba(255,255,255,0.9);
      padding: 8px 12px;
      border-radius: 4px;
      font-family: sans-serif;
      box-shadow: 0 1px 4px rgba(0,0,0,0.3);
    }
    button { margin: 2px; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

<div id="map"></div>

<div id="controls">
  <button id="fly-to-paris">Fly to Paris</button>
  <button id="fly-to-sydney">Fly to Sydney</button>
  <button id="fly-to-nyc">Fly to NYC (zoom‑in)</button>
</div>

<!-- MapLibre GL JS -->
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.2/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script src="app.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

---

## 2. JavaScript (`app.js`)

```js
// ---------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Initialise the map
// ---------------------------------------------------
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',                     // id of the div above
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any valid style URL
  center: [-0.1276, 51.5074],           // London (lon, lat)
  zoom: 5,
  pitch: 0,
  bearing: 0
});

// Add navigation controls (optional but handy)
map.addControl(new maplibregl.NavigationControl());

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣  Define the destinations you want to fly to
// ---------------------------------------------------
const destinations = {
  paris: {
    center: [2.3522, 48.8566],   // lon, lat
    zoom: 12,
    bearing: 0,
    pitch: 45,
    // duration is in ms – 2000 = 2 seconds
    duration: 2000,
    // optional easing function (default is a smooth easeOut)
    // easing: t => t   // linear (for demo)
  },

  sydney: {
    center: [151.2093, -33.8688],
    zoom: 13,
    bearing: -30,
    pitch: 60,
    duration: 2500
  },

  nyc: {
    center: [-74.0060, 40.7128],
    zoom: 14,
    bearing: 0,
    pitch: 0,
    duration: 1500
  }
};

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣  Helper that runs the fly animation
// ---------------------------------------------------
function flyToLocation(target) {
  // Guard against calling flyTo while a previous animation is still running.
  // map.isMoving() returns true while the map is animating.
  if (map.isMoving()) {
    // You could cancel the current animation with map.stop() if you prefer.
    // map.stop();
    console.warn('Map is already moving – ignoring extra click.');
    return;
  }

  map.flyTo({
    center: target.center,
    zoom: target.zoom,
    bearing: target.bearing,
    pitch: target.pitch,
    duration: target.duration,
    // `essential: true` tells assistive tech that the animation is
    // required for the user experience (required for the "prefers-reduced-motion"
    // media query handling in MapLibre v4+).
    essential: true,
    // You can also pass a custom easing function here if you want.
    // easing: t => t // linear
  });

  // Optional: do something when the animation finishes
  map.once('moveend', () => {
    console.log('Arrived at destination:', target.center);
  });
}

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 4️⃣  Wire the buttons
// ---------------------------------------------------
document.getElementById('fly-to-paris').addEventListener('click', () => {
  flyToLocation(destinations.paris);
});

document.getElementById('fly-to-sydney').addEventListener('click', () => {
  flyToLocation(destinations.sydney);
});

document.getElementById('fly-to-nyc').addEventListener('click', () => {
  flyToLocation(destinations.nyc);
});
```

---

## 3. What `map.flyTo()` Does (and why it’s “smooth”)

| Option | Meaning | Typical values |
|--------|---------|----------------|
| `center` | `[lon, lat]` of the new focal point | `[-74.006, 40.7128]` |
| `zoom`   | Target zoom level (higher = closer) | `12‑15` |
| `bearing`| Rotation of the map, degrees clockwise from north | `0‑360` |
| `pitch`  | Tilt of the map, degrees from the horizon | `0‑60` (higher = more 3‑D) |
| `duration`| How long the animation lasts (ms) | `1000‑3000` |
| `essential`| Marks the animation as essential for accessibility (required for the “prefers‑reduced‑motion” media query) | `true` |
| `easing`| Custom easing function (`t => number` where `t` ∈ [0,1]) | default is a smooth ease‑out; you can supply `t => t` for linear, etc. |

`flyTo` works by interpolating **both** the geographic position *and* the zoom level along a curved “flight path”. The algorithm (originally from Mapbox GL) automatically chooses a speed that feels natural: it speeds up, then slows down as it approaches the target.

If you only need a simple pan/zoom without the “flight” feel, you can use `map.easeTo()` instead – it still animates but follows a straight line.

---

## 4. Common Gotchas & Tips

| Issue | Fix / Tip |
|-------|-----------|
| **Button spams cause jitter** | Disable the button while `map.isMoving()` is true, or call `map.stop()` before a new `flyTo`. |
| **User has “prefers‑reduced‑motion” enabled** | MapLibre automatically respects this if you set `essential: true`. If you want to *skip* the animation entirely, check `window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)')` and call `map.jumpTo()` instead. |
| **Need to run code after the fly finishes** | Listen for the `moveend` event (or `zoomend`/`rotateend` if you only care about one aspect). Use `map.once('moveend', ...)` so the handler runs just once. |
| **Changing the style while flying** | Call `map.setStyle()` *after* the animation completes, or pause the animation with `map.stop()` first. |
| **Animating many points (e.g., a tour)** | Chain `flyTo` calls inside the `moveend` handler or use `async/await` with a Promise wrapper around `flyTo`. Example: <br>`await new Promise(r => map.once('moveend', r));` |

---

## 5. Full‑screen Minimal Demo (Paste into a single HTML file)

If you just want to test it instantly, copy everything below into a file called `fly-demo.html` and open it in a browser:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre FlyTo Demo</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.2/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body,html{margin:0;height:100%;}
    #map{position:absolute;top:0;bottom:0;left:0;right:0;}
    #controls{
      position:absolute;top:10px;left:10px;
      background:#fff;opacity:.9;padding:8px 12px;
      border-radius:4px;font-family:sans-serif;
      box-shadow:0 1px 4px rgba(0,0,0,.3);
    }
    button{margin:2px;}
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<div id="controls">
  <button id="paris">Paris</button>
  <button id="sydney">Sydney</button>
  <button id="nyc">NYC</button>
</div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.2/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
    center: [-0.1276, 51.5074],
    zoom: 5
  });
  map.addControl(new maplibregl.NavigationControl());

  const places = {
    paris: {center:[2.3522,48.8566], zoom:12, bearing:0, pitch:45, duration:2000},
    sydney:{center:[151.2093,-33.8688], zoom:13, bearing:-30, pitch:60, duration:2500},
    nyc:{center:[-74.0060,40.7128], zoom:14, bearing:0, pitch:0, duration:1500}
  };

  function flyTo(place) {
    if (map.isMoving()) return; // ignore extra clicks
    map.flyTo({...places[place], essential:true});
    map.once('moveend',()=>console.log('Arrived at',place));
  }

  document.getElementById('paris').onclick  =()=>flyTo('paris');
  document.getElementById('sydney').onclick =()=>flyTo('sydney');
  document.getElementById('nyc').onclick    =()=>flyTo('nyc');
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the file, click any button, and watch the camera glide smoothly to the new location.

---

### TL;DR

1. **Create the map** (`new maplibregl.Map`).
2. **Define the target view** (center, zoom, bearing, pitch, duration).
3. **On button click**, call `map.flyTo({ …targetOptions, essential:true })`.
4. Optionally listen for `moveend` to run post‑flight logic.

That’s all you need to give your users a polished, animated “fly‑to” experience in MapLibre GL JS! 🚀
